reviewer.reply:
{
    "verdict": "reject",
    "blockers": [
        {
            "claim": "Memory leak: session is captured by value in lambda but never destroyed on error path. The lambda captures 'session' directly, but if connect fails (err is not empty), the session is never destroyed, causing a memory leak.",
            "file": "app/qttunebridge.cpp",
            "quote": "QMetaObject::invokeMethod(this, [this, uri, err, session]() {"
        },
        {
            "claim": "Thread safety violation: m_pendingSession was removed but the comment in the original code explained it was atomic for thread-safe handoff between connect thread and GUI thread. The new approach captures session directly in the lambda but if the bridge is destroyed before the lambda runs, the captured session becomes an orphaned resource.",
            "file": "app/qttunebridge.cpp",
            "quote": "qttune_session_t* session = nullptr;\nconst QString err = connectCoreOnly(uri, &session);"
        },
        {
            "claim": "Lifetime hazard: the lambda captures session by value but there's no guarantee the session will be cleaned up if the bridge is destroyed. The original design used m_pendingSession atomic to ensure the destructor could find and destroy any pending session. This safety mechanism was removed without a replacement.",
            "file": "app/qttunebridge.cpp",
            "quote": "if (qttune_session_t* pending = m_pendingSession.exchange(nullptr)) {\n        qttune_session_destroy(pending);\n    }"
        }
    ],
    "questions": [
        "How does the code ensure the session is destroyed if the lambda is dropped because 'this' (the bridge) is destroyed before the queued invocation runs?",
        "What happens to the session if qttune_session_start succeeds but the lambda never runs (bridge destroyed)?"
    ],
    "nits": [
        "The comment update from 'Creates and registers, the slow part...' to 'Creates, registers, starts' is accurate for the new behavior",
        "The removal of startSession() simplifies the code as intended"
    ]
}
